.TH TERMINAL-PUPPETEER 1 "2026-08-24" "terminal-puppeteer 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
terminal-puppeteer \- drive a real interactive program through a pty, keystroke by keystroke
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B terminal-puppeteer.py
.BR \-\-ssh " " \fIHOST\fR
[\fB\-\-ssh\-arg\fR \fIARG\fR] ...
.RB [ \-\-env " " \fIKEY=VALUE\fR] ...
.RB [ \-\-steps\-file " " \fIPATH\fR]
.RB [ \-\-step\-delay " " \fISECONDS\fR]
.RB [ \-\-settle " " \fISECONDS\fR]
.RB [ \-\-connect\-wait " " \fISECONDS\fR]
.RB [ \-\-raw ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RI [ STEP " ...]"
.br
.B terminal-puppeteer.py
.BR \-\-cmd " " \fICOMMAND\fR
.RB [ \-\-env " " \fIKEY=VALUE\fR] ...
.RB [ \-\-steps\-file " " \fIPATH\fR]
.RB [ \-\-step\-delay " " \fISECONDS\fR]
.RB [ \-\-settle " " \fISECONDS\fR]
.RB [ \-\-connect\-wait " " \fISECONDS\fR]
.RB [ \-\-raw ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RI [ STEP " ...]"
.br
.B terminal-puppeteer.py
.B \-\-list\-keys
.br
.B terminal-puppeteer.py
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B terminal-puppeteer.py
opens a real pseudo-terminal (pty), spawns a target program attached to
it, and sends the target actual keystrokes with delays between them, the
way a person typing at a real terminal would \-\- then returns everything
the pty produced as one transcript.
.PP
It exists for a class of bug a plain, non-interactive tool call cannot
reach:
.TP
.B no pty
Without a pty, a remote shell's \fB$\-\fR never contains \fBi\fR (nothing
gated on \fB[[ $\- == *i* ]]\fR runs), and plenty of local programs detect
a non-tty stdin/stdout and change behavior outright.
.B \-\-ssh
and
.B \-\-cmd
both allocate a real pty, not a pipe.
.TP
.B one shot
\fBssh host 'cmd'\fR or a single non-interactive tool call sends one
command and reads one reply. Bugs that depend on what's already in
flight \-\- a live auto-suggestion mid-keystroke, a background job, one
key landing while another is still being processed \-\- need actual
keystrokes sent while the target process keeps running, not a full line
handed over at once.
.TP
.B bulk paste != typing
Writing a whole multi-line string to the pty in one shot is
.I not
the same as a human typing it: without bracketed-paste framing, each
embedded newline submits whatever line precedes it right then, so a
multi-line function definition sent as one write arrives as several
broken partial commands. Keep multi-line payloads to a
.B "source /path/to/file"
step instead of typing the file's contents \-\- see \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.SH TARGET
Exactly one of \fB\-\-ssh\fR or \fB\-\-cmd\fR is required.
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh " HOST"
Shorthand for \fB\-\-cmd "ssh \-tt HOST"\fR.
.B \-\-ssh\-arg
adds an extra argument to the underlying \fBssh\fR invocation (repeatable,
only valid with \fB\-\-ssh\fR) \-\- e.g. two instances of
\fB\-\-ssh\-arg\fR to pass \fB\-o BatchMode=yes\fR for a non-interactive
auth check.
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " COMMAND"
Any command, shell-quoted as one string and split with Python's
\fBshlex.split\fR (so quote it the way you'd type it:
\fB\-\-cmd 'bash \-i'\fR,
\fB\-\-cmd 'tmux attach \-t work'\fR).
Runs local to wherever \fBterminal-puppeteer.py\fR itself runs.
Shell-agnostic by construction \-\- nothing bash-specific anywhere in
this tool, so
.B \-\-cmd 'zsh \-i'
works the same way (see
.B NOTES.md
for what a live-redraw line editor's own output looks like in a flat
capture).
.SH STEPS
Everything after the target is a flat list of
.IR STEP s,
applied in order. A step is either literal text, sent to the pty exactly
as written with no added newline, or one of a closed set of named keys
(matched case-insensitively):
.TS
allbox tab(:);
lb l.
Name:Sends
TAB:\\t
ENTER, RET, CR:\\r
NEWLINE:\\n
ESC:\\x1b
BACKSPACE:\\x7f
UP, DOWN:\\x1b[A , \\x1b[B
LEFT, RIGHT:\\x1b[D , \\x1b[C
HOME, END:\\x1b[H , \\x1b[F
DELETE:\\x1b[3~
PGUP, PGDN:\\x1b[5~ , \\x1b[6~
CTRL\-\fIletter\fR:any A\-Z, e.g. CTRL\-C = \\x03
.TE
.PP
.B \-\-list\-keys
prints this same table (from the same data the tool itself matches
against, not transcribed separately here) and exits.
.B CTRL\-\fIletter\fR
is not a fixed handful \-\- any letter A\-Z works
(\fBCTRL\-U\fR, \fBCTRL\-L\fR, \fBCTRL\-W\fR, ...), computed the same way
a real terminal computes a control character.
.PP
A step that doesn't match any known key is still sent as literal text,
same as always \-\- but if it's close to one (a missing hyphen, a typo)
a warning is printed to standard error saying so. Advisory only: what
gets sent is unchanged either way.
.PP
.B No step ever gets an implicit trailing newline.
That is what lets a step be a bare \fBTAB\fR or a partial line someone's
about to keep typing \-\- but it also means a command meant to actually
run needs its own explicit \fBENTER\fR step, or it just sits typed and
unsubmitted for the rest of the run: \fB'git status' ENTER\fR, not
\fB'git status'\fR alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-steps\-file " PATH"
Append more steps, one per line, on top of any given on the command
line \-\- blank lines skipped, \fB#\fR-prefixed lines are comments,
.I PATH
of
.B \-
reads from standard input instead of a file. Useful once a repro has
more steps than comfortable as shell arguments, or needs to be
versioned/reused.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.BI \-\-env " KEY=VALUE"
(repeatable) sets an extra environment variable for the target process,
merged onto the inherited environment. Most useful for something that
has to be true
.I before
the target starts, which a step can't guarantee:
.B \-\-env TERM=dumb
avoids some terminal-capability probing a program checking
.B $TERM
might otherwise do on startup (see
.B CAVEATS
and
.B NOTES.md
in the project repository) \-\- an early
.B "'export TERM=dumb' ENTER"
step sets the same variable, but only after the shell is already up and
reading input, racing whatever probe happens first.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message (usage synopsis plus every option below) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-keys
Print the table of named keys (see \fBSTEPS\fR) and exit. Needs neither
\fB\-\-ssh\fR/\fB\-\-cmd\fR nor any \fISTEP\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-env " KEY=VALUE"
Extra environment variable for the target process, merged onto the
inherited environment (repeatable; not a replacement \-\- \fBPATH\fR and
everything else the target would normally see is still there). See
\fBENVIRONMENT\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-step\-delay " SECONDS"
Seconds to wait for output after each step, before sending the next one
(default: \fB1.5\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-settle " SECONDS"
Extra seconds to wait after the last step, for anything still catching
up (default: \fB1.5\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-connect\-wait " SECONDS"
Seconds to wait for a banner or shell prompt before the first step
(default: \fB3.0\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Print output with ANSI escape sequences intact, instead of the default
of stripping them.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each step to standard error as it is sent (\fB>> TAB\fR), for
watching a long or slow run happen live instead of waiting for the whole
transcript at the end. Standard output is unaffected either way.
.SH TIMING
Every step is followed by up to \fB\-\-step\-delay\fR seconds of draining
pty output before the next one is sent; \fB\-\-connect\-wait\fR is the
same kind of wait before the
.I first
step, to let a login banner or shell prompt show up; \fB\-\-settle\fR is
one more wait after the
.I last
step. All three stop early once output goes quiet for 200ms, so raising
them costs nothing when the target responds fast \-\- the defaults exist
for the slow case.
.PP
Raise \fB\-\-step\-delay\fR/\fB\-\-settle\fR when the output looks
truncated or the next step's echo appears mashed into the previous
step's output; that is usually the delay running out before the target
actually finished responding, not a real ordering bug.
.SH OUTPUT
Output is everything the pty produced, ANSI escape sequences stripped by
default (\fB\-\-raw\fR to keep them), written to standard output followed
by a trailing newline. Read it the way you'd read a terminal transcript:
prompt lines and command output interleaved, in the order they arrived.
.SH "EXIT BEHAVIOR"
On its way out this
.B only ever terminates the target process
(SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 5 seconds if it's still alive) \-\- it never
types anything to close it. There is no single "graceful quit" keystroke
that is correct across every target (\fBexit\fR+\fBENTER\fR for a shell,
\fBCTRL\-D\fR for a REPL, a different key entirely for \fBtmux\fR), so
this does not guess. Include whatever quit step actually applies to your
target as the last entry in your own step list if a clean logout matters
for what you're checking.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The target ran to completion (or was terminated on schedule) and output
was captured and printed.
.TP
.B 2
A command-line usage error \-\- no target given, both \fB\-\-ssh\fR and
\fB\-\-cmd\fR given, no steps given, a malformed \fB\-\-cmd\fR, a
\fB\-\-steps\-file\fR that doesn't exist, or similar. A one-line message
plus the usage synopsis is printed to standard error.
.TP
.B 1
The command named by \fB\-\-cmd\fR (or \fBssh\fR itself, for \fB\-\-ssh\fR)
could not be started \-\- not found, not executable, or similar. A
one-line message is printed to standard error, no usage block. Any other
unexpected error still prints a Python traceback.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Drive a remote shell over SSH, the case this tool was built for:
.PP
.RS
.EX
terminal-puppeteer.py \-\-ssh myhost 'git co' TAB CTRL\-C
.EE
.RE
.PP
Any local interactive program, not just a remote shell:
.PP
.RS
.EX
terminal-puppeteer.py \-\-cmd 'bash \-i' 'echo $\-' ENTER
terminal-puppeteer.py \-\-cmd 'tmux attach \-t work' 'echo hi' ENTER
.EE
.RE
.PP
A scripted multi-step repro, read from a file instead of argv:
.PP
.RS
.EX
terminal-puppeteer.py \-\-ssh myhost \-\-steps\-file repro.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Defining something multi-line: write it to a file first, then source it
as a single step, rather than typing the file's contents:
.PP
.RS
.EX
terminal-puppeteer.py \-\-cmd 'bash \-i' 'source /tmp/repro.sh' ENTER
.EE
.RE
.PP
Avoiding some terminal-capability probing before it can even happen
(see \fBCAVEATS\fR), and checking what a key name actually sends:
.PP
.RS
.EX
terminal-puppeteer.py \-\-ssh myhost \-\-env TERM=dumb 'echo hi' ENTER
terminal-puppeteer.py \-\-list\-keys
.EE
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
This tool's pty has no terminal emulator behind it, so escape sequences
a target sends to probe terminal capabilities (cursor position reports,
device attribute queries) go unanswered \-\- raw, unhandled bytes sitting
at the start of a capture against a program that probes on startup
(ble.sh, for one) is expected, not a bug in whatever is actually being
tested. Setting
.B \-\-env TERM=dumb
avoids some of this for programs that check
.B $TERM
before deciding whether to probe at all \-\- in the target's environment
from the moment it starts, unlike the same variable set via an early
step, which still races whatever probing happens before that step
lands. A byte that renders invisible in a terminal transcript (a
control byte with no glyph) is still there and can still matter; when a
captured value needs to be byte-exact, pipe it through \fBod\fR(1) rather
than trusting what printed. See
.B NOTES.md
in the project repository for the fuller account, including a real bug
this cost real time on.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I PATH
given to \fB\-\-steps\-file\fR: one step per line, blank lines and
\fB#\fR-prefixed comment lines skipped, everything else stripped of
surrounding whitespace and treated exactly like a positional \fISTEP\fR.
.I PATH
of
.B \-
reads standard input instead.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ssh (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR pty (7)
.PP
.B README.md,
.B NOTES.md,
and
.B DEVLOG.md
in the project repository: what the tool does and how to invoke it,
gotchas from actually using it against a real bug, and a running record
of what changed in the project and why, respectively.
.SH BUGS
Report issues at
.BR https://github.com/NireBryce/terminal\-puppeteer/issues .
.SH AUTHOR
Originally built by an agent (Claude Code) to reproduce a ble.sh/bash
completion bug that only ever showed up while actually typing at a real
